# Version 0.4.76
################################################################################

DRAM's distill stopped with "EmptyDataError: No columns to parse from file" when it found no rRNAs or no tRNAs in the
genomes of an assembly, which happens easily on small test data. DRAM writes no table in that case, the rule before
leaves an empty file, and distill can't read that. Both distill rules now only give DRAM the tables that have something
in them, and their logs say which ones they left out.

genome_stats.tsv then has no columns for rRNAs or tRNAs, and no assembly quality, for that assembly. To check that DRAM
really found none, look for "No rRNAs were detected" in the annotate log of that assembly.

Nothing to change in your project. Snakemake runs the failed distill job again by itself.

# Version 0.4.75
################################################################################

The defaults are for CSC's Roihu now. Puhti and Mahti were retired on 15 October 2026, and on their values Roihu stops jobs
with "Requested node configuration is not available".

Change your project: it has its own copy of config/config.yaml, and of the profile and the run script, and an update of the
pipeline doesn't touch them. In your project's config/config.yaml, take over from the pipeline's copy:

   tmp_storage, nvme_storage and the whole block resource_sets

In your project's profile, remove the line with gres. In your run script, load Snakemake with these two lines, or keep yours
if it works:

   module load bio-apps/v202603
   module load snakemake/9.14.0

What changed, and why:

- No set asks for --gres=nvme, as on Roihu this only exists on the hugemem nodes. All jobs get a local disk in $TMPDIR
  without asking, with 20 GiB in small and longrun, and 600 GiB in medium. The sets with _nvme in their name stay, so that
  config/escalation.yaml still fits, and they're now the same as those without it.
- The local disk is $TMPDIR, as CSC asks. $LOCAL_SCRATCH isn't there in a normal job on Roihu, and as rules run in strict
  mode, an unset variable stops them.
- Time limits fit the partitions: longrun and highmem_longrun have 10 days instead of 14.
- highmem and highmem_longrun run in small and longrun, whose large nodes take 1500 GiB. In the hugemem partitions the memory
  depends on the number of cores, and 20 cores can't have 1.4 TB.
- full_node is a whole node of the partition medium, with 384 cores and 36 hours, and it costs 288 billing units per hour.
  Only Kraken2 uses it.
- The eggNOG rules only copy the database if it fits into the local disk, and otherwise leave it where it is.

Roihu's default quotas are 250 GiB of scratch and 500,000 files, and extensions don't move over from Puhti. Check them with
csc-workspaces, as the databases alone need far more than that.

# Version 0.4.74
################################################################################

This fixes four bugs. Results of MaxBin2, Nonpareil and eggNOG-mapper on MAGs from earlier versions aren't what they
should be, and neither is what follows from MaxBin2's bins. On a small test with real data, with 1 million read pairs per
sample, these fixes had a real effect:

- Nonpareil with X: 100 gave no model for two of three libraries. With X: 1000, all three got one, and the sequencing needed
  for 95% coverage of the first library went from 17 Gb to 47 Gb.
- MaxBin2's bins changed, and with them the only genome of the catalogue: 588 contigs instead of 554, from another binner.
- metaSPAdes made identical contigs, so its fix doesn't change an assembly.

The fix of eggNOG-mapper hasn't run on real data yet, as its database doesn't fit on the test computer.

Change your project: Nonpareil's X is in your project's config/params.yaml, which an update of the pipeline doesn't touch.
To use the new value, change this line:

   read_annotate:
     nonpareil:
       X: 1000

The other three fixes are in the rules, and you get them with the update:

- MaxBin2 got the number of covered bases of a contig as its abundance, so a longer contig got a higher value. It now gets
  the mean depth. Snakemake runs MaxBin2 again, and everything after it: MAGScoT, dRep, and all that uses the catalogue.
- mag_annotate__eggnog had --no_annot, so it stopped after the search, and the MAG catalogue had no functional annotation.
  Now you get eggnog.emapper.annotations. If that folder exists already, remove it first, as its log is protected.
- metaSPAdes got its memory limit in MB and read that as GB, so it never stopped by itself, and SLURM killed the job
  instead. It now stops at the limit of the resource set, and the next set takes over. A rule's retry works as before.

# Version 0.4.73
################################################################################

Running a module now also makes its report, as its last job. So this makes Rreports/reads.pdf as well:

   bash run_Snakebite-Holoruminant-MetaG.sh reads

A single tool's target, such as read_annotate__kraken2, doesn't make a report. The targets report and report_<module> work
as before.

The rules of the tools didn't change, so this shouldn't make finished jobs run again. If a report's job itself fails, the
module counts as failed, although its results are there. You can then make the report again with report_<module>.

# Version 0.4.72
################################################################################

The reports were tested on real output of more tools, and this changes three things. No action is needed.

- A file in an unexpected format now costs only the section of its tool. Before, an error while reading stopped the whole
  report. sylph writes HIGH in its column Eff_lambda at high coverage, and this stopped the report of read_annotate.
- The table of DIAMOND has two counts now, reads with a hit and labels. Before, it showed the labels as hits, which is too
  high when a protein has several labels.
- The table of sylph has no column "Reads explained" anymore, as this value is always 100%. It has the median effective
  coverage instead.

The report of read_annotate also shows the numbers and taxa of phyloFlash now, and not only a list of its HTML files.

# Version 0.4.71
################################################################################

Every module now has a report, Rreports/<module>.pdf, and the rule all makes them at the end of a run. docs/06-Details.md
describes what's in them. The module eukaryote is optional, so its report has its own target, report_eukaryote.

If you only want the reports of a finished project, run the target report:

   bash run_Snakebite-Holoruminant-MetaG.sh report

The reports need a new container, rocker/verse, which Snakemake downloads once (1.3 GB). A project set up with an
earlier version doesn't need a change, but you can add this line to its config/.docker.yml to choose another version:

   report: "docker://rocker/verse:4.4.2"

Reports written by an earlier version have mistakes, so don't use their numbers: bins and genes were counted too high,
host removal was always empty, and CoverM's tables were missing. They also could not be built in their container.

Before you apply a suggestion for config/escalation.yaml, check that the run had data like your next one. A test on a
subset says little about the memory that the full data needs.

# Version 0.4.70
################################################################################

Check results/read_annotate/nonpareil/nonpareil.tsv in your projects. If its columns are empty, delete the file, and
