# Version 0.4.69
################################################################################

Please check your clean reads and replace your Krona charts, if you have results from an earlier version.

Clean reads can be truncated. preprocess__bowtie2__extract_nonhost__run wrote its files through ">(pigz ...)", and Bash
doesn't wait for such a process. The job could end while pigz was still writing, and nothing reported it. How often this
happened depends on the system and its load. In a run in a container, where everything stops with the main process, it
happened every time. A truncated file can lose reads at its end, and depending on the tool that reads it, this gives an
error or goes unnoticed. To check your clean reads:

   find results/preprocess/bowtie2/decontaminated_reads -name '*.gz' -exec sh -c 'gzip -t "$1" 2>/dev/null || echo "broken: $1"' _ {} \;

If that finds a broken file, delete it and everything built on it, and let Snakemake make them again. A project without
host genomes is not affected, as its clean reads come from fastp. The per-read output of Kraken2 (results/read_annotate/
kraken2/*/*.out.gz) had the same problem, but no rule reads it, and the reports are not affected.

The Krona charts of earlier versions are wrong, so don't use them. The rule gave a Kraken2 report to Krona with options for
the per-read output, so read counts were taken for taxon numbers. The Kraken2 reports themselves are fine. Snakemake makes
new charts by itself, as the rule changed:

   bash run_Snakebite-Holoruminant-MetaG.sh read_annotate__krona

For an existing project folder:

1. Several rules changed, and one of them is at the start of nearly everything. Snakemake notices this and would rerun
   the finished rules. If your clean reads passed the check above, continue the project with the option

   --rerun-triggers mtime

   but this doesn't make new Krona charts by itself, so delete the old ones in results/read_annotate/krona first.

2. In your project's config/escalation.yaml, rename assemble__bowtie2__build to assemble__bowtie2__build__run. Otherwise
   that rule gets the default escalation (small, medium, large).

The DIAMOND rules behave differently in two situations. If a database fits neither into /dev/shm nor on the local disk, the
last attempt now runs from the original place, as intended. Before, the rule stopped with an error if it had a single
resource set. And copy_dbs: "False" with diamond_shm: "True" no longer makes the job fail after DIAMOND has finished.

# Version 0.4.68
################################################################################

Please check your results if you ran a version with the bug described here. It affects runs from the branch
standalone-rewrite since 8 September 2026 (commit 31c40e2), and from main since 3 November 2026 until this fix reached
it. That includes main's commit 33daa47, which already calls itself 0.4.71.

In those versions, rules ran without "set -euo pipefail". A command that failed inside a rule didn't stop it, and
only the last command decided about success. So a tool could crash or run out of memory, or a pipe could break, and
the job still counted as COMPLETED, possibly with an incomplete or empty result, and with later rules building on
that. Rules that end in the tool's own command were not affected by this when that tool failed, but many rules do
something after the tool, such as moving a log file.

The rule's log file holds the message of the failed tool, so you can search for it. This finds candidates, and many
tools also use these words in normal messages, so you have to look at what it finds:

   grep -rilE 'error|killed|out of memory|segmentation fault|cannot allocate' results --include='*.log*'

Also check that compressed results are intact, and look for files that are suspiciously small:

   find results -name '*.gz' -exec sh -c 'gzip -t "$1" 2>/dev/null || echo "broken: $1"' _ {} \;
   find results -type f -size -100c -not -name '*.log*' -not -name '*.tsv'

If you find a broken result, delete it and everything built on it, and let Snakemake make them again.

Now that failures count again, a rule may fail where it used to pass. That is the intended effect, and it means the
earlier result of that rule was probably not trustworthy.

The resource escalation works differently now, which you may notice in a running project:

- A job that fails is retried as a new SLURM job with the next resource set of config/escalation.yaml. Before, a
  failing tool was first retried inside the same SLURM job, while its command claimed more memory and CPUs than
  the job really had. You can recognise those old retries by "Trying to restart" in the logs under
  .snakemake/slurm_logs/.
- Every resource set of a rule gets one attempt.
- A rule with a single resource set in config/escalation.yaml is no longer retried at all. Before, it got one more
  try in the same job. This applies to most rules in the default file, so give a rule several resource sets if you
  want it to escalate, for example "small, medium, large".

# Version 0.4.67
################################################################################

The rules of several modules were reworked, while all output files stay at the same paths with the same
content. For an existing project folder:

1. Snakemake notices the changed code and would otherwise rerun the finished rules, so always do a dry run first
   and continue the project with the option

   --rerun-triggers mtime

2. Some rules have new names, which your project's config/escalation.yaml has to follow. Rules that are not listed
   there get the default escalation (small, medium, large).

   assemble__magscot__hmmsearch_pfam and _tigr   ->  assemble__magscot__hmmsearch
   assemble__magscot__join_hmms                  ->  assemble__magscot__marker_table
   assemble__magscot__reformat and _rename       ->  assemble__magscot__label_contigs
   assemble__drep__separate_bins                 ->  assemble__drep__split_bins
   assemble__drep__join_genomes                  ->  assemble__drep__catalogue

3. The memory_gb and bowtie2-build entries of params.yaml and the virify entry of features.yaml were never read.
   You can remove them from your copies, but they do no harm.

# Version 0.3.8
################################################################################

The file structure for the non-host reads has changed in this version, so that, upon rerun, the pipeline 
will most likely regenerate the entire workflow, when you ran it prior to this release on your data. To 
avoid that behavior, you would first need to run the preprocess__bowtie2 rules like this

bash run_Pipeline-Holoruminant-meta.sh preprocess__bowtie2

to generate a the new files from the structe (and also get the quantification for the chromosomes) followed by 

bash run_Pipeline-Holoruminant-meta.sh --touch 

to adjust the files to the new structe. After that, the pipeline should be again aware of the files.

And as always, newer start the pipline without a dry-run after an update on an existing project!!!